In plant cells, a contractile ring cannot pinch the rigid outer wall. Instead, cytokinesis relies on the assembly of a cell plate. Microtubules guide Golgi-derived vesicles to the center of the dividing cell, where they fuse together. These vesicles deliver membrane components and cell wall precursors, expanding outward until the new cell plate reaches the edges of the parent cell and fuses with the existing membrane and wall, dividing the cytoplasm in two.
